            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to flow, then the Latin for to swim across."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: stagnum, stagnī",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "stagnum, stagnī — \"a pool, a pond; standing water\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In silvā stagnum quiētum est, et rānae cantant. In the wood there is a quiet pool, and frogs sing."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Second declension, neuter: stagnum, stagnī n. Still water, not running. English stagnant comes from the verb stagnāre."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The origin of stagnum is not settled: Lewis & Short compare Greek tenagos 'shallow water', but EtymDB gives no parent. English stagnant comes from stagnāre, built on it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
