          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "gurges, gurgitis — \"a whirlpool, an abyss of water; a flood\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nauta in gurgitem cadit, sed amīcī eum servant. A sailor falls into a whirlpool, but his friends save him."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Third declension, masculine: gurges, gurgitis m. Also a glutton, one who swallows everything. English regurgitate comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "gurges is inherited and Lewis & Short link it with gula 'throat'; EtymDB lists Greek gorgyra 'underground drain' as a cousin. English regurgitate comes from a word built on it."
            }
          ]
        },
